134
00:08:28,401 --> 00:08:31,560
Now I'm going to bring the angle of my saw up to about 45 degrees.

135
00:08:31,561 --> 00:08:36,060
And I'm going to start sawing.

136
00:08:36,061 --> 00:08:42,360
I'll lift the saw slightly as I go on the reverse stroke so the teeth aren't dragging.

137
00:08:42,361 --> 00:08:45,360
Keep sawing until I get right near the end.

138
00:08:45,361 --> 00:08:50,080
When I get this close, what I'm going to do is I'm going to hold this piece.

139
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:52,575
I'm going to slow down.

140
00:08:52,576 --> 00:08:57,580
I'm going to saw very gently at the very end to avoid tearing out the corner.

141
00:08:58,440 --> 00:08:59,260
There's my cut.

142
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:01,680
But not all saws cut on the push stroke.

143
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,840
Japanese saws all cut on the pull stroke.

144
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:09,200
Japanese saws are very, very thin.

145
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:15,100
When you have a thinner saw, that means you have to remove less wood to make the cut, so they cut faster.

146
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:20,360
Western saws have thicker blades, so they won't buckle on the more powerful push stroke.

147
00:09:20,361 --> 00:09:25,360
Japanese pull saws are designed to cut softer woods.

148
00:09:25,361 --> 00:09:31,360
The reason we want to work this low, we want to be able to use our foot to hold the work piece down,

149
00:09:31,361 --> 00:09:35,360
and then use both our arms on the saw to get lots of power to the cut.

150
00:09:35,361 --> 00:09:40,360
Now at the end of the cut, I slow down a little tiny bit, and it comes right off.

151
00:09:40,361 --> 00:09:41,061
off.

