but the heart and soul of the mill, the saws,

238
00:15:44,381 --> 00:15:47,960
are thinner, sharper, stronger, and faster.

239
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:54,220
The Dahlstrom Lumber Mill in Hoquiam, Washington is a big log mill.

240
00:15:54,221 --> 00:15:57,180
It handles, well, big logs.

241
00:15:57,181 --> 00:16:01,180
Generally that means a log maybe over 20 inches on the small end.

242
00:16:01,181 --> 00:16:04,460
We can run up to five foot from the log through our mill.

243
00:16:04,461 --> 00:16:07,900
As these large western hemlocks roll into the mill,

244
00:16:07,901 --> 00:16:10,500
the first saw they face is a deck saw.

245
00:16:10,501 --> 00:16:16,340
The deck saw we have here is a chainsaw, basically like every chainsaw.

246
00:16:16,341 --> 00:16:18,240
This one has bigger teeth on it.

247
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:19,780
It's got an 8-foot bar on it.

248
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,600
It's capable of cutting logs up to over 5 feet.

249
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:27,380
The chainsaw bucks the logs, or in layman's terms, cuts them to length.

250
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:31,580
Then the operator runs them through the debarker,

251
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:34,040
which preps them for the head rim,

252
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:37,940
a steel bandsaw that does most of the job of sawing logs into lumber.

253
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:41,400
The band is actually 35 feet in length.

254
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:43,940
The rim speed, or the speed of the blade,

255
00:16:43,941 --> 00:16:47,100
is about 8,800 feet a minute or 100 miles an hour.

256
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:51,300
This large bandsaw turning on six-foot diameter wheels

257
00:16:51,301 --> 00:16:52,460
is the workhorse of the mill.

258
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:55,600
It's large enough to cut the big logs.

259
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:57,860
It's more energy efficient than a circular saw,

260
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:02,400
and it slices a thinner kerf than a circular saw,

261
00:17:03,020 --> 00:17:04,260
cutting back on wood waste.

262
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:10,060
As the log approaches, the head sawyer attempts to slice

263
00:17:10,061 --> 00:17:12,000
the most high-grade or appearance-grade lumber

264
00:17:12,001 --> 00:17:13,360
out of the outside of the log.

265
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:17,940
A laser helps the sawyer to place the bandsaw.

266
00:17:17,941 --> 00:17:21,940
The cans, sliced boards that have not been edged,

267
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:25,940
roll down the conveyor belt towards the edger.

268
00:17:25,941 --> 00:17:29,940
Every saw in the edger is identified on that table with a laser light,

269
00:17:29,941 --> 00:17:33,940
so the operator knows if he sends that piece of wood in there exactly where it would be cut.

270
00:17:33,941 --> 00:17:37,940
A 250-horsepower motor drives a bank of six circular saws,

271
00:17:37,941 --> 00:17:41,940
spinning at 1,750 RPM.

272
00:17:41,941 --> 00:17:46,940
The blades are expensive and require constant maintenance.

273
00:17:46,941 --> 00:17:52,940
Friction builds heat, and heat is like the enemy of saw blades or steel.

274
00:17:52,941 --> 00:17:54,940
It warps and distorts it.

275
00:17:54,941 --> 00:17:56,940
So you've got to keep your saw blades cool.

276
00:17:56,941 --> 00:17:59,940
Our band mills and our edger are water-cooled as they're sawing.

277
00:17:59,941 --> 00:18:03,940
Water is sprayed on the blades to help keep them cool.

278
00:18:03,941 --> 00:18:07,940
In addition, carbide-cutting teeth, which are wider than the plate,

279
00:18:07,941 --> 00:18:13,040
than the plate, carve out kerf for the saw to spin.

280
00:18:13,041 --> 00:18:15,980
Friction is also the enemy of the bandsaw.

281
00:18:15,981 --> 00:18:19,320
When you get a bandsaw hot, it can possibly leave the wheel.

282
00:18:19,321 --> 00:18:21,620
It distorts it so bad, and it can walk off the wheel.

283
00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:26,440
To maintain the bandsaws, a saw filer

284
00:18:26,441 --> 00:18:28,540
sharpens and widens the teeth so that they'll

285
00:18:28,541 --> 00:18:30,760
cut a broad enough kerf for the blade to run cleanly.

286
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:36,300
This is called swedging.

287
00:18:36,301 --> 00:18:40,280
It's how we make the saw teeth wider than the plate of the saw.

288
00:18:40,860 --> 00:18:43,920
This turns and it forces the metal up to the point of the tooth,

289
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:46,980
which makes it spread out and wider than the plate.

290
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:52,280
And then we shape it back and then grind it sharp and it's ready to go.

291
00:18:57,960 --> 00:18:59,780
With the help of their dog, Marius,

292
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:04,420
John and Laurel Robarge run the blade maintenance shop at Dahlstrom Lumber.

293
00:19:04,421 --> 00:19:07,360
As they sharpen the blades over several months,

294
00:19:07,361 --> 00:19:09,260
they will grind the width of each blade down

295
00:19:09,261 --> 00:19:11,860
by a couple of inches.

296
00:19:11,861 --> 00:19:14,460
This process extends the life of the blades,

297
00:19:14,461 --> 00:19:18,900
but eventually makes them too narrow to use.

298
00:19:18,901 --> 00:19:21,400
Heat also causes blades to warp.

299
00:19:21,401 --> 00:19:24,440
You'll get these hot spots and it'll expand,

300
00:19:24,441 --> 00:19:26,540
and then your saw starts wobbling in there.

301
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:32,920
And then we get them after that, and you just sit

302
00:19:32,921 --> 00:19:37,800
and you bench them until the lumps are out and the teeth are sharp.

303
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:41,180
Benching is basically what I'm doing on this anvil,

304
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:44,260
making sure that if you've got high spots,

305
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:45,980
that you've got to pound them back down.

306
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:49,220
As the lumber leaves the mill,

307
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:52,660
it's on its way to becoming an essential part of some kind of structure.

308
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:56,140
But one day that structure may face remodeling,

309
00:19:56,680 --> 00:20:01,200
and that time comes an entirely different saw,

310
00:20:01,201 --> 00:20:03,560
the Milwaukee Sawzall will be there.

311
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:06,940
The Sawzall is a reciprocating saw,

312
00:20:07,380 --> 00:20:10,580
a power saw with a blade that moves back and forth during cutting.

313
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:15,940
The Sawzall's aim is to live up to its name.

314
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:18,580
What can't you use a Sawzall for?

315
00:20:18,680 --> 00:20:23,500
From cutting birthday cake, to lumber, to demolition, to fire and rescue,

316
00:20:24,340 --> 00:20:26,860
to, I even knew a guy, he talked about,

317
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:28,780
he wanted to get rid of a stump,

318
00:20:28,781 --> 00:20:33,960
So he actually used it to cut down in the ground to cut the roots of the stump so he could pull it out.

319
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:39,580
The super sawzall is the top of the Milwaukee line and includes orbital cutting action.

320
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:43,600
Normally a reciprocating action is straight back and forth.

321
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:46,980
Orbital cutting is more of a circular cut. It's a much more aggressive cut.

322
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:52,420
We have a unique feature to Milwaukee. It's a 360-degree rotating handle.

323
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:57,280
This is nice because it allows the contractor to adjust the handle to the most comfortable position.

324
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:02,060
It generates 0 to 3,000 strokes per minute.

325
00:21:02,460 --> 00:21:05,060
So it's literally the fastest reciprocating saw on the market.

326
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:09,460
The Sawzall has no problem cutting through wood, screws, and nails.

327
00:21:10,620 --> 00:21:12,520
Even the steel hinges on this door.

328
00:21:13,620 --> 00:21:16,560
The Sawzall was introduced in 1951

329
00:21:16,561 --> 00:21:19,560
and originally developed for plumbers and electricians.

330
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:21,640
And actually what they were looking for

331
00:21:21,641 --> 00:21:25,860
was a compact, durable, lightweight solution

332
00:21:25,861 --> 00:21:28,260
to cut basically anything that they ran into.

333
00:21:28,780 --> 00:21:33,780
The original Sawzall was a heavy all-metal tool.

334
00:21:33,781 --> 00:21:39,780
Today's version incorporates plastics and nylons for lighter weight and vibration dampening.

335
00:21:39,781 --> 00:21:47,780
This super Sawzall and its blades are made at a Milwaukee electric tool plant, which is not in Milwaukee.

336
00:21:47,781 --> 00:21:53,780
In Greenwood, Mississippi, the heart of the Mississippi Delta, catfish and cotton.

337
00:21:53,781 --> 00:21:57,780
The building itself is 150,000 square feet.

338
00:21:57,781 --> 00:22:01,780
We make tens of thousands of sawzaws a year.

339
00:22:01,781 --> 00:22:05,780
The key to making a machine that saws just about anything is manufacturing blades for

340
00:22:05,781 --> 00:22:07,780
all types of materials.

341
00:22:07,781 --> 00:22:11,780
The blade we're looking at now is for metal cutting.

342
00:22:11,781 --> 00:22:14,780
And the reason we know that is because the teeth are finer.

343
00:22:14,781 --> 00:22:22,780
There's more TPI, or teeth per inch, because you don't want to have an as aggressive bite

344
00:22:22,781 --> 00:22:24,880
when you're cutting metal.

345
00:22:24,881 --> 00:22:27,760
Comparing it to wood, you can take a heavier cut,

346
00:22:27,761 --> 00:22:30,660
a larger chip, because the wood is softer.

347
00:22:30,661 --> 00:22:33,920
But on metal, you have to have a finer tooth.

348
00:22:33,921 --> 00:22:37,000
As you see on this blade, the tooth is much different

349
00:22:37,001 --> 00:22:37,960
from the previous one.

350
00:22:37,961 --> 00:22:39,260
It's much larger.

351
00:22:39,261 --> 00:22:40,940
The angles are different.

352
00:22:40,941 --> 00:22:42,740
The depth of the gullet is different.

353
00:22:42,741 --> 00:22:45,560
And this one is designed for a very aggressive

354
00:22:45,561 --> 00:22:47,480
sheetrock and plaster cutting.

355
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:50,380
There were no saws involved

356
00:22:50,381 --> 00:22:52,440
in the manufacture of saw blades.

357
00:22:52,441 --> 00:22:53,440
teeth are ground in.

358
00:22:53,441 --> 00:22:59,440
The blade process begins with the uncoiler, with a coil of steel.

359
00:22:59,441 --> 00:23:04,440
The uncoiler uncoils it into the straightener.

360
00:23:04,441 --> 00:23:09,440
The blade material is straightened prior to going into the press.

361
00:23:09,441 --> 00:23:16,440
Once it gets into the press, the blades are stamped out to the configuration of the blade

362
00:23:16,441 --> 00:23:17,440
itself.

363
00:23:17,441 --> 00:23:23,360
Once they are loaded off of the press, the blades are accumulated into handfuls for the robot.

364
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:27,760
The robot swings over, picks it up, and loads it into the grinder.

365
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:34,000
A ceramic grinding wheel grinds the saw's tooth pattern into a pack of 90 blades.

366
00:23:36,580 --> 00:23:42,580
The grinding wheel forms and sharpens the teeth as a mixture of water and lubricant washes metal filings away.

367
00:23:42,581 --> 00:23:51,220
Workers then load the blades into furnaces to temper the metal in order to retain its hardness and its flexibility.

368
00:23:58,260 --> 00:24:02,360
The National Cathedral, the Empire State Building, the Pentagon,

369
00:24:03,240 --> 00:24:06,440
they all have one thing in common, Indiana limestone.

370
00:24:07,080 --> 00:24:11,100
What's unique about Indiana limestone is its ability to be worked.

371
00:24:11,101 --> 00:24:14,800
It's a soft stone relative to limestone.

372
00:24:14,801 --> 00:24:17,240
It's very consistent in color.

373
00:24:17,241 --> 00:24:20,840
For that reason, it's been chosen as a building stone.

374
00:24:20,841 --> 00:24:23,080
The stone projects an aura of permanence,

375
00:24:23,081 --> 00:24:25,880
stateliness, calm.

376
00:24:25,881 --> 00:24:28,260
But today, the process of cutting it into blocks

377
00:24:28,261 --> 00:24:29,260
is anything but calm.

378
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:37,100
At the quarry, the first cut is the deepest.

379
00:24:37,101 --> 00:24:41,100
In this case, 15 feet down into the limestone bed.

380
00:24:41,101 --> 00:24:45,580
The saw for the job? A W. F. Myers diamond belt saw.

381
00:24:45,581 --> 00:24:48,380
Though designed specifically for the task of quarrying stone,

382
00:24:48,381 --> 00:24:50,140
it resembles another popular saw.

383
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:59,980
Just picture a chainsaw, for example, and we put a belt around a bar that is in geometry

384
00:24:59,981 --> 00:25:02,565
very similar to a handheld chainsaw.

385
00:25:02,566 --> 00:25:06,300
Instead of using a chain, we use a belt that has the

386
00:25:06,301 --> 00:25:08,552
diamond segments and the plastic around it.

387
00:25:08,553 --> 00:25:10,700
The belt saw's diamond studded teeth will

388
00:25:10,701 --> 00:25:14,280
will slice out almost 30,000 square feet of limestone

389
00:25:14,281 --> 00:25:19,400
before needing replacement after 375 hours of operation.

390
00:25:19,401 --> 00:25:22,680
Cutting with diamonds is a simple principle.

391
00:25:22,681 --> 00:25:25,400
To cut something hard, it takes something harder.

392
00:25:25,401 --> 00:25:28,520
And diamonds are one of the hardest materials known to man.

393
00:25:28,521 --> 00:25:31,880
Diamonds themselves are the cutting tool.

394
00:25:31,881 --> 00:25:33,480
They're just like the teeth on a saw.

395
00:25:33,481 --> 00:25:35,600
Powder is itself what holds the diamonds in,

396
00:25:35,601 --> 00:25:37,600
but the diamonds do the cutting.

397
00:25:37,601 --> 00:25:40,020
The powder in this case, 20% carbide

398
00:25:40,021 --> 00:25:43,140
and 80% cobalt is mixed with the diamonds,

399
00:25:43,141 --> 00:25:45,800
cold pressed into shape, and then hot pressed

400
00:25:45,801 --> 00:25:48,240
to form a hardened segment.

401
00:25:48,241 --> 00:25:51,260
As these diamond-infused segments cut stone,

402
00:25:51,261 --> 00:25:54,260
the cobalt carbide alloy slowly wears away,

403
00:25:54,261 --> 00:25:57,340
continually exposing fresh diamonds at the cutting surface.

404
00:25:57,341 --> 00:26:02,480
The diamonds sit in through the segment.

405
00:26:02,481 --> 00:26:04,920
And as this segment wears, the diamonds

406
00:26:04,921 --> 00:26:09,760
stay sharp on the surface of the segment all the way through.

407
00:26:09,761 --> 00:26:15,580
The diamond segments are then brazed, or soldered at high temperatures, to steel mounts.

408
00:26:15,581 --> 00:26:21,780
Heated to 1200 degrees, the molten silver solder fuses the segment to the steel mount.

409
00:26:21,781 --> 00:26:23,720
It gets cherry red.

410
00:26:23,721 --> 00:26:28,220
It has to to melt the silver, and you've got to work it around and make sure that you have

411
00:26:28,221 --> 00:26:32,160
a good surface on each one so that it holds good.

412
00:26:32,161 --> 00:26:38,200
Because as you probably could figure, it takes a pretty good hold, because that stone, whenever

413
00:26:38,201 --> 00:26:41,120
it hits that stone, that's what does the cutting of the stone.

414
00:26:41,980 --> 00:26:46,320
The segments will cut everything from limestone to granite to marble to slate.

415
00:26:47,300 --> 00:26:51,200
The segments are sharp, but they wouldn't stay that way without lots of water.

416
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:55,686
The belt saw guzzles 50 gallons a minute.

417
00:26:55,687 --> 00:26:58,800
It pressurizes the water in a labyrinthine system

418
00:26:58,801 --> 00:27:04,480
inside the hollow guide bar and shoots it out beneath the belt at 50 pounds per square inch.

419
00:27:04,481 --> 00:27:08,140
The belt hydroplanes on the pressurized water, reducing wear.

420
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:12,380
It also allows the cuttings that we've created to be flushed out

421
00:27:12,381 --> 00:27:15,860
so that we're not constantly recutting the same old stone

422
00:27:15,861 --> 00:27:18,440
and prematurely wearing out the belt.

423
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:23,520
The computer-controlled saw will cut a 15-foot-deep,

424
00:27:23,640 --> 00:27:26,100
70-foot-long channel in eight hours.

425
00:27:26,980 --> 00:27:28,020
It's an auto saw.

426
00:27:28,460 --> 00:27:30,200
It'll program it right there in that cut,

427
00:27:30,340 --> 00:27:32,740
and it'll go down all on its own automatically.

428
00:27:32,980 --> 00:27:34,900
Never, never have to fool with it.

429
00:27:35,860 --> 00:27:38,020
After the saw has completed the vertical cut,

430
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:42,840
Workers drill horizontal holes and hydraulically split the rock along the bottom,

431
00:27:43,280 --> 00:27:48,380
removing the limestone in 50-foot-long, 5-foot-wide, 15-foot-deep sections.

432
00:27:50,180 --> 00:27:52,560
Though the Myers quarry saw as cutting edge,

433
00:27:53,480 --> 00:27:56,400
it's merely the most modern application of stone-cutting techniques

434
00:27:56,401 --> 00:27:58,680
first practiced thousands of years ago.

435
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:04,600
The most prolific ancient stone-cutters were the Egyptians,

436
00:28:04,601 --> 00:28:06,720
who worked extensively with limestone.

437
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:15,200
Metal saws fashioned of copper, bronze, and sometimes iron were used with sand or emery,

438
00:28:15,201 --> 00:28:21,200
a cutting grit like modern day diamonds, to fashion blocks of limestone over 6,000 years ago.

439
00:28:21,201 --> 00:28:28,200
In order to get the abrasive particles to stick to the edge, they'd use a resinous substance of some kind,

440
00:28:28,201 --> 00:28:35,200
you know, plant resins, to get those particles against the stone or the material that they were trying to cut.

441
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:45,280
In stone mills, a modern version of this abrasive-fed cutting technique was widespread before the

442
00:28:45,281 --> 00:28:50,540
advent of diamond-studded cutting teeth in the 1960s.

443
00:28:50,541 --> 00:28:53,494
One hundred years ago, gang saws like these at B.J.

444
00:28:53,495 --> 00:28:55,580
Hoadley Quarries would have been fed

445
00:28:55,581 --> 00:29:01,960
with an abrasive slurry of sand or silica to enable them to grind through limestone.

446
00:29:01,961 --> 00:29:06,120
Today these old-fashioned saws are still at work, but they've been updated with diamond

447
00:29:06,121 --> 00:29:11,120
This is a rocking gang, what they call a lift gang.

448
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:13,720
And it lifts and hits, lifts and hits.

449
00:29:13,820 --> 00:29:16,360
And the slurry gets trapped in between the segments

450
00:29:16,460 --> 00:29:17,860
and in the middle.

451
00:29:17,960 --> 00:29:20,360
Because when it's cutting, the first one will cut.

452
00:29:20,460 --> 00:29:23,280
Then the slurry will get trapped in between the segments.

453
00:29:23,380 --> 00:29:26,980
And then the second one has a cleaner spot to cut.

454
00:29:27,080 --> 00:29:29,880
And it just carries it all out to the end of the blade

455
00:29:29,980 --> 00:29:32,380
with water.

456
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:36,120
At Indiana Limestone Company's fully modern mill,

457
00:29:36,121 --> 00:29:41,720
The job of slabbing stone block from their quarry is accomplished by gigantic diamond belt saws.

458
00:29:43,080 --> 00:29:46,760
The blocks come directly from the quarry looking just like this,

459
00:29:46,761 --> 00:29:51,040
and the first real operation of fabrication that we do is to slab them.

460
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:54,080
We use these narrow belt saws.

461
00:29:54,760 --> 00:30:00,140
Cutting 14 inches per minute, this saw slices a block into seven slabs in one hour.

462
00:30:00,141 --> 00:30:06,940
It works much like the quarry belt saw, but uses a narrower cutting belt.

463
00:30:06,941 --> 00:30:13,540
The belt travels around in a circle through the two big wheels, hydroplaning across it.

464
00:30:13,541 --> 00:30:18,220
It's about washing the slurry out, allowing the diamond to dig next and dig next, and

465
00:30:18,221 --> 00:30:23,140
if the slurry is there, it can't do that.

466
00:30:23,141 --> 00:30:28,040
Once blocks have been slabbed, they'll run through an assembly line of 40, 48, and 64-inch

467
00:30:28,041 --> 00:30:28,940
circular saws.

468
00:30:28,941 --> 00:30:34,340
Tipped with diamond cutting teeth, I'm running at a surface speed of 10,000 feet per

469
00:30:34,341 --> 00:30:36,980
minute. The stone is cut to last.

470
00:30:36,981 --> 00:30:42,100
Phil Byers and Eddie Brooking have been brazing diamond teeth to

471
00:30:42,101 --> 00:30:47,660
saws for more than 80 combined years. Well, we have a lot of mills in this area,

472
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:50,723
but we ship all over the United States.

473
00:30:50,724 --> 00:30:53,240
Not only here, it's everywhere, you know, Canada,

474
00:30:53,241 --> 00:30:58,385
everywhere. But this is what they call the home of the limestone.

475
00:30:58,386 --> 00:31:00,760
Limestone capital of the world

476
00:31:00,761 --> 00:31:03,448
is what they bill it as. Rightly so.

477
00:31:03,449 --> 00:31:07,480
Indiana's limestone is of the highest quality and most

478
00:31:07,481 --> 00:31:12,020
plentiful in the world. The mill and quarry saws get down and dirty every day.

479
00:31:13,560 --> 00:31:18,120
When you look at a saw, what it's designed to cut is not always obvious.

480
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:22,240
The basic wood saws, most of us recognize them.

481
00:31:23,020 --> 00:31:25,600
Stone saws, a little less common and harder to place.

482
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:31,800
But this saw, with a surprising amount of power and speed,

483
00:31:32,800 --> 00:31:34,980
it must be for something fairly serious.

484
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:37,460
And it is.

485
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:43,140
This is the M-Power 6300 oscillating saw, designed to cut bone.

486
00:31:43,141 --> 00:31:49,300
A surgeon turns to the saw for large bone surgeries, like total hip and knee replacement,

487
00:31:49,301 --> 00:31:55,100
or for more delicate procedures, like anterior cruciate ligament or ACL repair.

488
00:31:55,101 --> 00:32:01,800
In several sporting activities, the anterior cruciate, or the front ligament, tends to be

489
00:32:01,801 --> 00:32:02,959
torn and can get torn.

490
00:32:02,960 --> 00:32:06,540
What we need to do, surgically speaking, is to replace that ligament

491
00:32:06,541 --> 00:32:09,600
with a separate tendon that we harvest from the patient.

492
00:32:09,601 --> 00:32:16,800
The replacement tendon will be anchored by two 10 by 25 millimeter bone grafts, harvested

493
00:32:16,801 --> 00:32:20,640
from the knee by the M-Power 6300.

494
00:32:20,641 --> 00:32:26,600
This cordless $17,000 steel aluminum plastic saw can accommodate over a hundred different

495
00:32:26,601 --> 00:32:32,160
types of blades, tipped with serrated teeth and covered in a hardened chrome coating to

496
00:32:32,161 --> 00:32:36,280
reduce friction when cutting bone.

497
00:32:36,281 --> 00:32:42,400
The saw's variable speed blade oscillating from a dead start to 14,000 cycles per minute

498
00:32:42,401 --> 00:32:44,600
offers the surgeon increased control.

499
00:32:44,601 --> 00:32:51,340
I can start it up slowly just to score the bone without much vibration or jump until

500
00:32:51,341 --> 00:32:56,280
I get it started and then I can go ahead and go for broke.

501
00:32:56,281 --> 00:33:00,840
The combination of a thin profile yet stiff blade allows the surgeon to plunge the saw's

502
00:33:00,841 --> 00:33:03,720
tip directly into bone.

503
00:33:03,721 --> 00:33:07,360
The saw blade has depth marks on it, and I can watch my depth marks as I cut.

504
00:33:11,260 --> 00:33:18,060
In an actual surgery, these bone grafts would be screwed in place to anchor the new tendon replacement for the torn anterior cruciate ligament.

505
00:33:20,480 --> 00:33:23,820
Ultimately, the greatest challenge for surgical saws comes after surgery,

506
00:33:24,340 --> 00:33:30,400
when they're cleaned and then run through an autoclave, a device that uses steam to sterilize equipment.

507
00:33:30,401 --> 00:33:34,900
The autoclaves run at about 272 degrees Fahrenheit,

508
00:33:34,901 --> 00:33:38,700
so looking at that, you have to have electronic components

509
00:33:38,701 --> 00:33:40,400
that can withstand that type of heat,

510
00:33:40,401 --> 00:33:44,200
go through there hundreds of cycles a year for each handpiece,

511
00:33:44,201 --> 00:33:47,700
and then still focusing on getting it to work each time.

512
00:33:49,700 --> 00:33:52,700
For all but the most recent history of medical saws,

513
00:33:52,701 --> 00:33:56,700
germs were unknown and sterilization was not practiced.

514
00:33:56,701 --> 00:34:01,920
The early physicians knew that cutting off a limb was really the only way to

515
00:34:01,921 --> 00:34:05,520
save a patient's life. There were no antibiotics, there was no nothing else

516
00:34:05,521 --> 00:34:09,740
you can do to avoid sepsis. So even going back to Greek and Roman times,

517
00:34:09,741 --> 00:34:16,100
amputation was an operation that was carried out. A surgeon would have given

518
00:34:16,101 --> 00:34:20,920
this 1730s German amputation saw only a cursory cleaning.

519
00:34:20,921 --> 00:34:26,500
Dentalization came in at the end of the 19th century, so essentially they would

520
00:34:26,501 --> 00:34:30,220
take this saw from patient to patient without cleaning it and that was one of

521
00:34:30,221 --> 00:34:34,480
the reasons why infection killed far more people than the surgery itself. The

522
00:34:34,481 --> 00:34:37,360
friend of the surgeon in those days was speed. The faster you could get the limb

523
00:34:37,361 --> 00:34:41,080
off the better because patient didn't tolerate it very well and it would be

524
00:34:41,081 --> 00:34:45,820
used to saw back and forth on a patient's arm or leg while two assistants or more

525
00:34:45,821 --> 00:34:51,400
were holding the patient down. Though this practice must have been brutal it's

526
00:34:51,401 --> 00:34:56,020
even harder to imagine the use of the earliest medical saws. Prehistoric stone

527
00:34:56,021 --> 00:35:00,640
instruments. The earliest civilizations, thousands of years ago, you saw like

528
00:35:00,641 --> 00:35:05,440
instruments made from flint stones actually to cut into bones and even do

529
00:35:05,441 --> 00:35:11,160
potentially brain surgery in the skull. Trepanation, sawing or boring holes in

530
00:35:11,161 --> 00:35:16,140
the skull, is one of the oldest surgeries dating back as far as 6,500

531
00:35:16,141 --> 00:35:21,060
years. The practice was widespread among the Inca and Maya and was accomplished

532
00:35:21,061 --> 00:35:25,580
by abrasion, cutting and drilling techniques. It's speculated that these

533
00:35:25,581 --> 00:35:31,120
cultures performed trepanation to treat head traumas suffered in battle or accidents, to

534
00:35:31,121 --> 00:35:36,820
relieve headaches for ritualistic reasons, and even perhaps to release evil spirits.

535
00:35:36,821 --> 00:35:44,540
Today, trepanation is still used in some neurosurgical procedures.

536
00:35:44,541 --> 00:35:49,620
This trepine set was handcrafted in Philadelphia in the 1840s and would have been used to treat

537
00:35:49,621 --> 00:35:54,520
hematomas, or bleeding on the brain.

538
00:35:54,521 --> 00:36:00,360
By cutting a hole in the skull, the surgeon allowed blood to exit, thus relieving pressure on the patient's brain.

539
00:36:01,300 --> 00:36:05,340
The way that the trephine would work is there was a central pin,

540
00:36:05,900 --> 00:36:10,900
and the pin would slide in and out to direct exactly where the cuts will go.

541
00:36:11,880 --> 00:36:19,220
The trephine would be turned, and the cut made would be circular to whatever depth was indicated.

542
00:36:19,221 --> 00:36:25,180
And then the skull flap was then lifted with an elevator, which is this instrument right here.

543
00:36:28,180 --> 00:36:34,140
Sawbones, carpenter, though it's unclear when these slang terms for doctors enter the lexicon,

544
00:36:34,880 --> 00:36:40,480
they derive from a surgical practice that, though ancient, came into its own as a result of modern warfare.

545
00:36:41,520 --> 00:36:47,080
Most Civil War amputation sets have multiple thin blades because they had to be traded out quite frequently.

546
00:36:47,680 --> 00:36:51,460
There were over 30,000 amputations in Union forces alone,

547
00:36:51,760 --> 00:36:55,080
everything from fingers, which the survival rate was quite good for,

548
00:36:55,420 --> 00:36:59,880
up to mid-thigh amputations, where the survival rate was less than 50%,

549
00:36:59,881 --> 00:37:03,180
both from bleeding and trauma of surgery and also infection,

550
00:37:03,181 --> 00:37:06,940
which obviously set in very commonly prior to the age of antibiotics.

551
00:37:08,200 --> 00:37:10,680
This early saw was called an osteotome.

552
00:37:11,140 --> 00:37:15,060
It was made in the 1830s by an orthopedist named Bernard Heine.

553
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:20,280
It is the first mechanical chainsaw.

554
00:37:20,281 --> 00:37:23,240
And it, as you can see, is a bit unwieldy to use.

555
00:37:23,340 --> 00:37:24,080
It's quite heavy.

556
00:37:24,540 --> 00:37:28,740
It has to be directed to a very specific point for the very small teeth.

557
00:37:29,220 --> 00:37:35,160
And it was so expensive to make and to maintain that I just don't think it survived very well.

558
00:37:36,940 --> 00:37:39,660
Medical saws come in a variety of shapes and sizes.

559
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:42,680
But they're not the only ones whose purpose is to save lives.

560
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:53,260
When it comes to life and death, you need a chainsaw that can cut through walls and

561
00:37:53,261 --> 00:37:58,180
roofs in any rescue situation.

562
00:37:58,181 --> 00:38:10,180
An earthquake, hurricane, or fire.

563
00:38:10,181 --> 00:38:13,180
Smoke is pouring from a burning building.

564
00:38:13,181 --> 00:38:14,680
A firefighter is trapped inside.

565
00:38:14,681 --> 00:38:16,880
I have a half a tank of air.

566
00:38:16,980 --> 00:38:20,080
Each passing moment cuts into his chances of survival.

567
00:38:20,180 --> 00:38:21,280
We're making entry.

568
00:38:25,820 --> 00:38:28,020
Though this is just a training exercise,

569
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:31,500
it highlights the importance of one piece of equipment,

570
00:38:31,600 --> 00:38:34,640
the steel magnum rescue saw.

571
00:38:34,740 --> 00:38:38,600
That saw, when it encounters something behind air conduit,

572
00:38:38,700 --> 00:38:41,100
nails, two-by-fours, four-by-fours, it

573
00:38:41,200 --> 00:38:44,680
cuts right through that with no problem at all.

574
00:38:44,681 --> 00:38:52,060
We went in there with a quick extraction and got him out here to safety.

575
00:38:52,061 --> 00:38:54,260
All was good.

576
00:38:54,261 --> 00:38:59,180
Designed exclusively for rescues, this saw can make repeated cuts through wood and metal.

577
00:38:59,181 --> 00:39:03,180
This durability is what makes it special.

578
00:39:03,181 --> 00:39:08,480
Though firefighters have used chainsaws since the 1950s, this specialized type of power

579
00:39:08,481 --> 00:39:12,180
saw evolved slowly.

580
00:39:12,181 --> 00:39:16,560
chains were prone to breaking when they encountered extreme heat and metals embedded in walls

581
00:39:16,561 --> 00:39:17,295
and roofs.

582
00:39:17,296 --> 00:39:23,540
So for decades, firefighters relied mostly on axes to chop out access and ventilation

583
00:39:23,541 --> 00:39:28,840
holes. But this process could eat up valuable time during a rescue effort.

584
00:39:31,080 --> 00:39:36,620
In 1992, faced with these obstacles, the Virginia Beach Fire Department sought the help of the

585
00:39:36,621 --> 00:39:40,220
world's largest chainsaw manufacturer, Steel.

586
00:39:41,940 --> 00:39:44,720
Steel's engineers designed a more durable saw

587
00:39:44,721 --> 00:39:46,240
that could withstand the extreme heat

588
00:39:46,241 --> 00:39:49,100
firefighters face when cutting ventilation holes.

589
00:39:51,360 --> 00:39:53,700
The best standard chains may have only a portion

590
00:39:53,701 --> 00:39:55,260
of the cutter dipped in carbide,

591
00:39:56,380 --> 00:39:58,500
whereas the cutting edge on the RDR,

592
00:39:58,501 --> 00:40:03,260
or rapid duro rescue chain, is entirely covered in carbide,

593
00:40:03,261 --> 00:40:05,620
which gives the teeth greater impact resistance.

594
00:40:06,620 --> 00:40:14,080
At the firefighters' request, saw designers also made modifications to the body of the

595
00:40:14,081 --> 00:40:15,080
chainsaw.

596
00:40:15,081 --> 00:40:18,960
Our structural firefighting gloves are big and they're thick.

597
00:40:18,961 --> 00:40:23,000
It's very difficult to grip the old starter handle, so they developed this force right

598
00:40:23,001 --> 00:40:24,000
here.

599
00:40:24,001 --> 00:40:26,280
It's very easy to grab with your gloved hand.

600
00:40:26,281 --> 00:40:28,840
Another thing that they did was this wrap around handle right here.

601
00:40:28,841 --> 00:40:31,460
This gives us the ability to use it right or left.

602
00:40:31,461 --> 00:40:33,240
Most chainsaws are right-handed saws.

603
00:40:33,241 --> 00:40:37,440
So we needed that flexibility up on a roof or in a house or somewhere.

604
00:40:37,560 --> 00:40:38,920
We needed right or left operation.

605
00:40:40,280 --> 00:40:42,920
Another thing that you see right here is this front guard.

606
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:45,940
Basically what happens is when you're cutting asphalt shingles,

607
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:47,380
roofs that have tar in there,

608
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:50,420
the tar will strike the exhaust system and stick.

609
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:53,860
So they develop this guard that you see right here.

610
00:40:53,861 --> 00:40:57,100
It's easy to clean up, protects us all, and works very well.

611
00:40:57,101 --> 00:41:04,100
They are sharp, powerful, and can be dangerous.

612
00:41:04,101 --> 00:41:06,100
And when they're doing their thing,

613
00:41:06,101 --> 00:41:08,100
it's often best to keep our distance.

614
00:41:08,101 --> 00:41:11,100
But they do our bidding,

615
00:41:11,101 --> 00:41:15,100
helping us to shape our environment,

616
00:41:15,101 --> 00:41:20,100
to build, to destroy, and even to save lives.

617
00:41:20,101 --> 00:41:22,100
And when push comes to shove,

618
00:41:22,101 --> 00:41:25,100
they can even make music.

